`coconeInf` sends a compatible sequence to the supremum, over all levels, of the values of a family of Scott maps at the corresponding coordinates. `coconeInf_apply` is that formula, by definition.

`coconeInf_climb` says that if each component equals the next component after the inclusion, then applying a later component after climbing from level n returns the component at n. The base climb is the identity. A successor is the inclusion after the climb already reached, and the cocone equation replaces the next component by the current one. `coconeInf_descend` says that applying an earlier component after descending from level n is at most the component at n. The base descent is the identity. A successor descends the retraction, the inductive hypothesis bounds it, the cocone equation rewrites the component as the next component at the inclusion of the retraction, and that inclusion lies below the element.

`coconeInf_comp_embInf` recovers the component at n after composing the mediating map with the stage embedding. At later coordinates the embedded element has climbed, so the value equals the component at n. At earlier coordinates it has descended, so the value is at most that component. The coordinate at n is the original element, so that component occurs in the supremum. `coconeInf_preservesDirectedSup` exchanges the level supremum with a directed supremum: evaluation preserves the directed supremum at each coordinate, each component preserves directed suprema, and the two suprema commute.
